A||||2||curtis||current draw||||5||
Z||000000||curtis||04-21-2001||03:12 PM||||Just replaced the battery after something drained it completely. Checked the current by disconnecting the positive, placing one meter lead on the positive post of the battery and the other lead to the positive cable. Leaving the negative cable connected to the battery. My meter is showing 0.12 amps. Is this the right way to check the current. What is the usually current draw of the battery when turned off from the computer memory and thingg like that?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mark||04-21-2001||04:20 PM||||Well Curtis, you peaked my curiosity.  First, your measurement technique is correct.  I checked my '86 GT and saw a momentary (&lt; 5 second) surge current of about 300 mA.  The current quickly settled down to only about 8 mA (.008 ampere).  Just thinking... is your trunk light off when you take your measurement?  If not, hold the switch off while you measure.  Now, the meter I used was very old and I wouldn't exactly trust what it tells me, however the inrush current did over-range my digital multimeter (200 mA range), so that 300 mA inrush may be for real. <P>In any event 0.12 amperes sounds a little too high.  That's equivalent to about 20 amp-hours in a weeks time.... which sounds like enough energy to really be noticed.<P>Try pulling fuses in an attempt to isolate the current draw.  If that doesn't turn anything up, disconnect the alternator connections and measure again.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||04-21-2001||04:27 PM||||Pretty much exactly what was said. Start pulling one fuse at a time, and check the current draw over the terminals in the fuse box. This way you can isolate the circut that is draining your battery. It could be a bad/shorting out alternator also. Usally you will hear a ticking/crackling sound comming from the alternator if it is the problem.||||reg||1
